{"notes": "I couldn't write these notes. My first attempt to use the research tool was rejected because the tool-use limit for this turn had already been reached. No searches ran and I didn't read any pages.\n\nYour rules say to report only what the pages actually say, with a URL for every point, and never to fill gaps from my own knowledge. Without any pages, I have nothing I can honestly report for Matthew 21:24–46, so I've left the notes blank rather than make anything up.\n\n**When the tool is available again, I plan to read these sources:**\n- **Verse by verse:** the Enduring Word page on Matthew 21; the Bible Hub pages for each verse (Ellicott, Gill, Pulpit Commentary, Meyer, Bengel); the StudyLight commentaries on Matthew 21; the Precept Austin Matthew 21 page if there is one.\n- **Points where Protestant traditions differ:** Wesley's *Explanatory Notes* on Matthew 21 (wesley.nnu.edu); Spurgeon and Ryle (spurgeon.org, ccel.org); Desiring God and GTY sermons on the two sons and the tenants; Ligonier and The Gospel Coalition on verse 43 (\"given to a people\"); LCMS and 1517 on law and gospel in the parables. The question likely to divide them most is whether verse 43 means the church replaces Israel.\n- **Other views:** NET Bible notes on verses 31–32, 42 and 44 (including the question of whether verse 44 was in the original text); Bible Odyssey on the parable of the tenants; Chrysostom's *Homilies on Matthew* 67–68 and Aquinas's *Catena Aurea* (newadvent.org, ccel.org); catholic.com and oca.org on the vineyard and the cornerstone. I'd include popular modern teaching only if those sites actually discuss it.\n\nIf you send the request again in a new message, I'll do the research and write the notes in the \"v. 24: … (URL)\" format you asked for.", "urls": ["http://classic.net.bible.org/bible.php?book=Mat&chapter=21", "https://bible.org/article/which-son-obeyed-his-father-textual-problem-matthew-2129-31", "https://bible.org/book/export/html/21287", "https://bibl